WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.720
React Native's new architecture relies on the JavaScript interface, or JSI, to expose a synchronous

2
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:14.000
execution environment. This creates an immediate constraint for iOS engineers. JSI is a pure C++

3
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:19.760
library. When React Native calls a native module through a turbo module, the callpath expects to

4
00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:25.920
interact directly with C++. This establishes a hard limit. Native Swift code cannot be called

5
00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:32.080
directly from C++. This diagram illustrates the isolation between the JSI runtime on the left

6
00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:37.840
and standard iOS business logic on the right. An execution path originating in C++ simply

7
00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:42.800
shatters against a red boundary line when attempting to reach Swift. Apple introduced C++

8
00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:49.360
interoperability in Swift 5.9. Many developers assume this natively bridges the gap. While Swift 5.9

9
00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:55.280
does allow some interoperability, it requires explicit annotations and covers only a subset of patterns.

10
00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:00.880
It cannot satisfy the dynamic runtime resolution that JSI expects. Specifically, Swift's

11
00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:07.280
interop fails when attempting to resolve get turbo module. The exact method React Native requires

12
00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:12.960
to locate the native instance. The objective C runtime, however, was designed to interoperate

13
00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:20.480
with both C++ and Swift simultaneously. This makes an objective C++ compilation unit identified

14
00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:26.480
by the .MM extension, the only sanctioned integration point. Because direct communication is

15
00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:33.360
impossible, developers must construct a precise, multi-layered architectural bridge, starting from C++

16
00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:39.040
and ending in Swift to achieve synchronous module execution. Building this bridge begins outside

17
00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:45.280
of Xcode, starting with defining a strict contract between JavaScript and native. This code schematic

18
00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:51.200
represents a TypeScript specification file. It serves as the universal contract outlining the exact

19
00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:56.960
inputs and outputs for your native module. React Native reads the specification using a tool called

20
00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:03.120
CodeGen, which executes automatically during the standard pod install workflow. CodeGen parses the

21
00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:10.160
TypeScript spec and generates a pure C++ interface file. Modifying the TypeScript spec requires

22
00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:15.920
rerunning pod install before building an Xcode to ensure these generated files update correctly.

23
00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:23.760
This generated C++ file contains the exact protocol that the iOS native code must eventually implement

24
00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:31.040
to satisfy the JavaScript execution. This auto-generated C++ interface dictates the inflexible target

25
00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:36.720
that strictly drives the rest of the entire iOS architectural implementation. To implement this

26
00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:42.160
protocol, we shift to the far side of the architecture to establish the actual native business logic

27
00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:48.480
written in Swift. This is a standard Swift helper class, acting as a thread-safe storage dictionary.

28
00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:54.320
Notice the at-objcb-embers declaration at the top of the class. Inside the class, a concurrent

29
00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:59.680
dispatch queue using a dot-barrier flag guarantees thread-safe reads and serialized rights for the

30
00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:06.240
modules data. The at-obj-members annotation exposes every method and property in this class to the

31
00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:13.200
objective C runtime, removing the need to tag individual lines. However, Swift classes, even with

32
00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:19.280
annotations, are not visible to objective C files by default. We expose the Swift class to the rest

33
00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:25.440
of the project by generating a project bridging header. The exception here is for custom pod libraries,

34
00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:31.440
where Xcode automatically generates a dash-swift.h header, completely bypassing the need for a manual

35
00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:37.920
bridging file. The architecture now has isolated thread-safe Swift business logic that is fully visible

36
00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:44.080
to the objective C runtime, ready to be connected to the C++ layer. This diagram shows the final

37
00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:50.160
central piece of the architecture, the objective C++ implementation file. It slots exactly into the

38
00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:56.400
center between the C++ protocol and the Swift logic. The dot-mem extension is non-negotiable here.

39
00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:01.760
It is the only compiler environment capable of hosting C++ headers and objective C code simultaneously.

40
00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:07.120
The first critical function within this file is registering the module with the react native

41
00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:13.600
runtime using the RCT export module macro. The string argument inside this macro must perfectly

42
00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:19.520
match the module name defined back in the TypeScript spec. The second, most crucial method required in the

43
00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:26.400
dot-mem file is getTurboModule. GetTurboModule must return a specific memory pointer, a standard

44
00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:32.160
shared pointer directed back to the Cogen generated C++ class. This pointer wraps the objective C

45
00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:38.240
implementation and exposes it back through JSI. Missing this method means JSI cannot locate the Swift

46
00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:43.920
logic, causing the JavaScript call to fall back to the legacy bridge or fail entirely. In modern

47
00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:48.960
react native versions, app delegate changes are largely unnecessary once this is done, due to auto

48
00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:55.120
discovery handled by RCT app setup utles. The dot-mem file serves as the indispensable translator.

49
00:04:55.120 --> 00:05:01.120
It is the only sanctioned environment capable of turning a synchronous JSI C++ call into an

50
00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:07.600
objective C execution that invokes Swift logic. This diagram displays a complete successful execution

51
00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:14.320
path from a JavaScript invocation flowing through the C++ JSI layer across the dot-mem bridge

52
00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:20.080
and arriving at a Swift dictionary mutation. Because this relies on a strict multi-layered chain,

53
00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:25.840
a single misconfiguration causes a catastrophic break at a specific layer. The first common failure

54
00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:31.520
is a JavaScript runtime error stating cannot find module. This points to a failure at the registration

55
00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:37.520
layer. Either code Gen was not run or the string inside RCT export module does not match the TypeScript

56
00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:43.920
exactly. The second major error is a compiler failure stating the class does not conform to protocol.

57
00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:49.600
This indicates a signature mismatch at the C++ boundary, meaning the objective C++ methods lack the

58
00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:55.040
precise parameters and return types demanded by the code Gen output. The third failure occurs when

59
00:05:55.040 --> 00:06:01.600
the module compiles successfully, but returns undefined at runtime. This is almost always caused by a

60
00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:07.600
null pointer in the getTurboModule method, which breaks the link back to JSI. When troubleshooting

61
00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:14.160
this, verify that RCT enableTurboModule is active in the app delegate to prevent the runtime from

62
00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:20.720
falling back to the legacy bridge. By strictly respecting JSI's C++ boundaries and utilizing the

63
00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:26.720
objective C++ bridging pattern, developers secure the ability to execute complex, thread-safe

64
00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:36.720
Swift logic synchronously within modern react native applications.

